{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01JymtiQKkGZJxzVqcmRfwc1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was completed:\n\n---\n\n**✅ Task: `07-014-latency-vs-throughput-for-llm-serving.md`**\n\n**Key content written:**\n\n- **Root cause framing:** LLM decode is memory-bandwidth-bound (~2 FLOPs/byte at batch=1 vs A100's 156 FLOPs/byte ceiling), so the GPU is severely underutilized at small batch sizes — and larger batches are the only way to push utilization up.\n\n- **The core tension:** Larger batches → higher GPU utilization → more throughput, but every request waits longer in the batch → higher TTFT and per-request latency. You're always on a curve.\n\n- **vLLM knobs table:** `--max-num-seqs`, `--max-num-batched-tokens`, `--speculative-model`, `--enable-prefix-caching`, tensor parallelism — each mapped to its latency vs throughput effect.\n\n- **Concrete examples:**\n  - *Interactive chat* (p95 TTFT < 800 ms): `max-num-seqs 32` + speculative decoding → ~650 ms at 45% GPU util\n  - *Batch eval pipeline*: `max-num-seqs 256` → 1,800 tok/s vs 300 tok/s — 6× throughput at 10× per-request latency\n\n- **4 specific pitfalls** including the common \"continuous batching eliminates the tradeoff\" misconception and the KV cache memory pressure lever that most candidates miss.\n\n**3 items remaining in Next:** Q15, Q16, then validate."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":362,"cache_read_input_tokens":77403,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":362,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"620ed5df-57c3-4463-85f7-f967df5ab407","uuid":"2ed0022c-e138-46fe-b316-dd1b75ead752"}
